st have  gone to play rain now were you  in this mind of rain when the camera  wasn't rolling  and did you kind of how was it hard was  it to get out of that when the movie was  done filming  yeah i mean it's it's interesting um  obviously too  i i will never have any  idea what it is actually like to live  with schizophrenia  and and to be in those shoes um but i  mean obviously it was  very heavy material um a lot of days  were very exhausting  um but i i do i feel like i treat  characters and rolls almost like shoes  if that makes sense so like when i leave  for the day  i put on my shoes and then i come home  and i take them off so that way i'm kind  of able to just  unwind myself at home and i don't have  to bring anything  back with me um but it also helped  obviously we had just a  fantastic cast and so like harry connick  jr was just  such a jokester all day so it was nice  to kind of have like the levity of like  some lightness in between some of the  dark scenes  okay so that's that's great that you  actually have that and speaking of his  character who plays your father  in the film can you kind of elaborate on  the line that kind of stuck with me the  most that you say to him in your big  fight scene with him  when you said i'm not my sickness can  you kind of just go a little more what  that exactly means when rayne's saying  that  yeah i mean it's i think that's kind of  the whole stigma with  with mental illness and and  schizophrenia just as schizophrenia is  you know it's people who live with  schizophrenia they're they're ordinary  people  they put on their shoes in the morning  and they brush their teeth  and they have a future rain especially  like  it's so interesting to play a young  woman with schizophrenia  which i feel like is something that's  not often told from a perspective of you  know a 15 year old girl  um because she has a normal life and and  she goes to school and she has crushes  on boys and she gets in fights with her  dad  and s